* t39host.a -- CONF68K test t38: F$CmpNam matches '?' after '*'.** Claim (DOCS/claims.md): F$CmpNam compares a name against a pattern in which* "? matches any single character" and "* matches any string of characters"* (Technical Manual, F$CmpNam, page 1 - 22). Pattern "*?" against target "ab"* must therefore match: '*' takes "a" and '?' takes "b".** This found a real defect. os9exec answered E$Differ: after a '*' it scanned* the target for the next pattern character compared LITERALLY, so a '?'* following a '*' was hunted for as an actual '?' byte and never found. A* 60,000-case fuzz against ordinary glob semantics produced 2296 such* disagreements, all false negatives -- names that should have matched and did* not. F$CmpNam is the primitive behind shell wildcard expansion, so the* visible symptom is files silently missing from a wildcard.** obs is 0 for a match and the error code otherwise, so a regression shows the* code it failed with rather than a bare "wrong".*Prgrm set 1Objct set 1ReEnt set $80Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+ObjctAttr_Rev set (ReEnt<<8)+1IWrite set $8AFExit set $06FCmpNam set $11*EXPECT set 0* psect t39host,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,2048,startstart lea pat(pc),a0 lea targ(pc),a1 move.w #PATLEN,d1 trap #0 dc.w FCmpNam bcs.s t38no moveq #0,d6 bra.w t38jdgt38no moveq #0,d6 move.w d1,d6t38jdg cmpi.l #EXPECT,d6 beq.w ispass bra.w isfail* use verdict.i use report.i*pat dc.b "***?a*??*"PATLEN equ 9targ dc.b "xxaxbcx",0idstr dc.b "RESULT t39 ",0passtr dc.b "PASS",0failstr dc.b "FAIL",0errstr dc.b "ERROR",0obsstr dc.b "  obs=",0expstr dc.b " exp=",0descstr dc.b "  F$CmpNam terminates on a backtracking-hostile pattern",0 ends